# Delineations for a planet occupying a given whole-sign house, keyed by
# [wsh_house][planet]. Each cell is {'Rhetorius': [entries], 'PN IV':
# {'Good': {'text', 'cite'}, 'Bad': {'text', 'cite'}}}. evaluate_planets_in_houses()
# prints the two PN IV halves in the page's two condition columns, 'If in a
# suitable condition' and 'If in a bad condition', each with its locator,
# and the entry list in a third column, 'Rhetorius and Firmicus, as the
# texts state it'.
#
# The Rhetorius list is this app's paraphrases of Rhetorius, Astrological
# Compendium Ch. 57 (Holden), the significations of the twelve houses, read
# house by house (he opens with the twelfth), and of Firmicus Maternus,
# Mathesis III.2-III.7 and III.13 (Dykes), the planets in the twelve places
# -- one tradition (Dykes's fn 28 to III.2 fills a lacuna from Rhetorius),
# but two witnesses, kept apart. Neither text divides a planet's reading
# into well and badly placed: they divide by sect and by conditions, so
# their testimony is a LIST of entries, not a pair of halves. Each entry is
# {'author', 'cite', 'axis', 'text', 'portional', 'conditional'}:
#   author  'Rhetorius' | 'Firmicus' | 'Rhetorius, as summarized by Dykes'
#           (the last for Dykes's fnn 284-285 to III.13, which supply
#           Rhetorius's Moon in the fifth and seventh where Firmicus's own
#           sentences, III.13, 19-22, are lost);
#   cite    Rhetorius by chapter, house and Holden's page or pages
#           ("Ch. 57, the sixth, p. 76"; "pp. 57-61" where the sentences run
#           over a page break or fold in the house's earlier general
#           sentences; "pp. 65, 88-89" where the paragraph the translators
#           reassign to this house sits on another house's pages); Firmicus
#           by chapter and sentence or run ("III.2, 8", "III.2, 4-5"); the
#           summaries "III.13 fn 284" / "III.13 fn 285";
#   axis    the author's OWN division, never converted: 'by day' / 'by
#           night' where he says so, 'in sect' / 'out of sect' where he says
#           so (Rhetorius), 'unsplit' where the sentence makes no such
#           division (a gradation such as "especially by day" stays in the
#           text), and 'general malefic' / 'general benefic' for Ch. 57's
#           "the malefics there ..." / "the benefics ..." sentences, entered
#           for Saturn and Mars, and for Jupiter and Venus, in that house;
#           'joint' for the four named pairs, sharing a testimony identity;
#   text    the paraphrase, its antecedents whole -- an entry keeps every
#           condition its sentence states or is left out whole; a
#           configuration that is not the planet's occupancy of the place
#           (the full Moon moving toward Mars) is not an entry;
#   portional  True where Firmicus states the placement "portionally" (in the
#           portion of the Hour-marker, portionally in the Nth place) -- or
#           where the cited run refers back to the place's opening sentence
#           that states it ("so put", "in this place", "in the Midheaven":
#           III.2, 4-5; 30; 41-43; 44-48; III.4, 22-25; 80-81), since dropping
#           the flag there would widen his scope silently; the reader prints
#           "(portionally)";
#   conditional  True where the entry's whole reading rests on a stated
#           configuration beyond the placement and the axis (Saturn in the
#           Hour-marker by day AND Mars in another pivot; Venus in the twelfth
#           joined by a ray with Jupiter); a cell whose entries run past
#           RHETORIUS_CELL_WORDS prints its other entries and sends these to
#           the row's detail, so that no condition is ever dropped to fit.
# Where the two authors disagree, both entries stand, each under its own
# name; no entry is a merged "both" cite. A sect word the translator
# supplies in brackets or angle brackets (Holden's "[by night]" for Saturn
# in the second, Dykes's "<by night>" for III.2, 8) is kept as the axis with
# the supplement named in the text. Where the translators mark a paragraph
# as printed under the wrong house (Holden's and Dykes's notes: Rhetorius's
# second set of third-house paragraphs is the ninth's, repeated there, and
# his second set of eleventh-house paragraphs for Saturn, Jupiter, Mars and
# Mercury is the fifth's), the paragraph serves the house it belongs to and
# is cited under it; Holden's fn 4 to p. 65 keeps the Sun paragraph there in
# the third, and the Venus paragraph there names the God or the Goddess, so
# both serve the third as well, with the note that its sect labels are the
# reverse of the ninth's copy. Rhetorius's Moon in the sixth, eighth and
# twelfth is lost in the manuscripts and Firmicus's Moon in the fifth to the
# eighth is missing; an empty list prints as a dash, which is the absence of
# testimony, not a neutral reading.
#
# The PN IV halves are this app's paraphrases of Abu Ma'shar's Book II
# chapters on the lord of the year in the houses of the circle -- Saturn
# II.6, Jupiter II.9, Mars II.12, the Sun II.15, Venus II.18, Mercury
# II.21 -- applied to natal planets, which is the TNAC Reference Guide's
# reading of those chapters and is followed here; the page says visibly
# that this is an adaptation of annual rules whose prerequisites (the root
# and the revolution together, the planet as lord of the year) the natal
# lookup does not establish. Each chapter's division is the planet's
# CONDITION -- suitable, received, in his own house, against alien, not
# received, made unfortunate, retrograde -- with the house among its
# factors, so the halves are 'If in a suitable condition' and 'If in a bad
# condition', and each keeps its own qualifications inside the cell
# (PN IV II.6, 15's retrograde alternative; II.6, 24's leisure inside the
# suitable reading; II.6, 1-3's for-himself / for-another distinction). Where a
# chapter groups houses (the eleventh or fifth, the ninth or third, the
# second or eighth, the sixth or twelfth, the four stakes) the group's
# sentence is cited for each house in it with the sharing named in the
# locator ("II.6, 4 (shared with the 5th)"). Saturn's four falling places
# (II.6, 22-24) are a common layer over his second, sixth, eighth and
# twelfth beside each house's own sentence, carried whole -- 22's "in the
# revolution", 23's intensifications, 24's residual leisure; Jupiter's four
# places not looking at the Ascendant (II.9, 15) and Venus falling from
# the stakes (II.18, 18) occupy Shared passages, outside the condition
# split; Jupiter II.9, 16 is a separate made-unfortunate addition. Mercury's II.21, 8 (the ninth or third)
# states no condition, unlike its adverse pair II.21, 9; the half says so.
# The Moon has no Book II houses chapter -- II.22, 13 says to judge her "in
# the manner of ... the rest of the planets", and the translator's fn 312
# points to VII.8, her transit through the twelve houses. VII.8 is a
# transit list with its own frame (the three positions, "so long as she is
# in it") and supplies no condition split, so it is NOT sorted into these
# two columns: the Moon's PN IV halves point to MOON_IN_HOUSES_VII8 below,
# her own unsplit table, one reading per house.
#
# The arrangement (a 12 x 7 grid) is the Guide's; tests/test_prose_tables.py
# pins every entry and every PN IV half to its sentence by locator and
# three shared anchor words.
def _half(text, cite=''):
    return {'text': text, 'cite': cite}
_DASH = _half('—')
def _P(good, gcite, bad, bcite, shared=()):
    return {'Good': _half(good, gcite) if good else _DASH,
            'Bad': _half(bad, bcite) if bad else _DASH, 'Shared': tuple(shared)}

RHETORIUS_AUTHORS = ('Rhetorius', 'Firmicus', 'Rhetorius, as summarized by Dykes')
RHETORIUS_AXES = ('by day', 'by night', 'in sect', 'out of sect', 'unsplit', 'general malefic', 'general benefic', 'joint')
_R, _F, _RD = RHETORIUS_AUTHORS
def _E(author, axis, cite, text, portional=False, conditional=False):
    assert author in RHETORIUS_AUTHORS and axis in RHETORIUS_AXES, (author, axis)
    return {'author': author, 'cite': cite, 'axis': axis, 'text': text,
            'portional': portional, 'conditional': conditional}

